\f0\i0  the other to happen; correlation only means the two variables move together statistically (a famous statistical warning)." \}, \{ "word": "association", "difference": "association is a looser, more general connection between two concepts or ideas in thought." \} ], "antonyms": ["independence", "disconnection", "disparity"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "correlation", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "correlate", "pos": "verb" \}, \{ "word": "correlative", "pos": "adjective" \} ], "usage_note": "A fundamental rule in statistics and science is: 'Correlation does not imply causation' (\uc0\u30456 \u38364 \u19981 \u31561 \u26044 \u22240 \u26524 ).", "frequency_rank": 96, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "math", "science"] \}, \{ "word": "corroborate", "pos": "verb", "phonetic": "/k\u601 \u712 r\u593 \u720 b\u601 re\u618 t/", "definition": "To confirm or give support to a statement, theory, or finding with evidence or authority.", "definition_zh": "\u35657 \u23526 \u65307 \u30906 \u35657 \u65307 \u25552 \u20379 \u35657 \u25818 \u25903 \u25345 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "com-", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "intensive prefix", "meaning_zh": "\u21152 \u24375 \u35486 \u27683 " \}, \{ "root": "robur / robustus", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "oak, strong", "meaning_zh": "\u27233 \u27193 \u12289 \u24375 \u22767 \u12289 \u22533 \u22266 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "COM\u65288 \u21152 \u24375 \u65289 + ROBUR\u65288 \u24375 \u22767 \u12289 \u22533 \u22266 \u65289 \u8594  \u25343 \u20986 \u35657 \u25818 \u20351 \u21407 \u26412 \u30340 \u35498 \u27861 \u35722 \u24471 \u28961 \u27604 \u22533 \u22266  \u8594  \u35657 \u23526 ", "story_en": "The witness was called to CORROBORATE the alibi, making the suspect's story look as ROBUST (strong) as an oak tree.", "story_zh": "\u35657 \u20154 \u34987 \u20659 \u21914 \u20358 \u12300 \u35657 \u23526 \u65288 CORROBORATE\u65289 \u12301 \u19981 \u22312 \u22580 \u35657 \u26126 \u65292 \u20351 \u23244 \u30097 \u20154 \u30340 \u35498 \u27861 \u30475 \u36215 \u20358 \u20687 \u27233 \u27193 \u19968 \u27171 \u22533 \u22266 \u65288 ROBUR\u65289 \u24375 \u22767 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55357 \u56540 ", "\u55358 \u56605 ", "\u9878 \u65039 "] \}, "example": "A second independent laboratory was hired to corroborate the results of the initial chemical experiment.", "example_zh": "\u32856 \u35531 \u20102 \u31532 \u20108 \u23478 \u29544 \u31435 \u23526 \u39511 \u23460 \u20358 \u35657 \u23526 \u26368 \u21021 \u21270 \u23416 \u23526 \u39511 \u30340 \u32080 \u26524 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "corroborate a story", "corroborate evidence", "independent corroboration", "fail to corroborate", "further corroborate" ], "synonyms": ["confirm", "substantiate", "verify", "validate", "authenticate"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "confirm", "difference": "confirm is a standard, daily word for verifying any statement or booking; corroborate is a formal legal/scientific word meaning to strengthen a hypothesis by adding independent, overlapping evidence." \}, \{ "word": "substantiate", "difference": "substantiate focuses on providing physical, concrete substance to back up an unproven claim." \} ], "antonyms": ["contradict", "refute", "deny", "disprove"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "corroborate", "pos": "verb" \}, \{ "word": "corroboration", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "corroborative", "pos": "adjective" \} ], "frequency_rank": 97, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "C1", "tags": ["college", "GRE", "law"] \}, \{ "word": "defer", "pos": "verb", "phonetic": "/d\u618 \u712 f\u604 \u720 r/", "definition": "To postpone or delay an action or event until a later time; to submit humbly to another's wish or authority.", "definition_zh": "\u25512 \u36978 \u65307 \u24310 \u26399 \u65307 \u32893 \u24478 \u65307 \u38918 \u24478 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "de-", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "away, down", "meaning_zh": "\u38626 \u38283 \u12289 \u21521 \u19979 " \}, \{ "root": "ferre", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "to carry, bring", "meaning_zh": "\u25884 \u24118 \u12289 \u36939 \u36865 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "DE\u65288 \u38626 \u38283 \u12289 \u19979 \u65289 + FERRE\u65288 \u25884 \u24118 \u65289 \u8594  \u25226 \u20107 \u24773 \u24448 \u24460 \u24118 \u36208  \u8594  \u24310 \u26399 \u65307 \u25110 \u32773 \u25918 \u19979 \u33258 \u24049 \u30340 \u24847 \u35211  \u8594  \u32893 \u24478 ", "story_en": "They decided to DEFER the game, CARRYing (ferre) the schedule DE- (away) to next weekend due to rain.", "story_zh": "\u30001 \u26044 \u19979 \u38632 \u65292 \u20182 \u20497 \u27770 \u23450 \u23559 \u27604 \u36093 \u12300 \u24310 \u26399 \u65288 DEFER\u65289 \u12301 \u65292 \u23559 \u26178 \u31243 \u34920 \u24478 \u26412 \u36913 \u26411 \u24118 \u36208 \u65288 FERRE/DE\u65289 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u9203 ", "\u55357 \u56787 \u65039 ", "\u55357 \u56903 \u8205 \u9794 \u65039 "] \}, "example": "The student requested to defer her university admission for a year to work and gain practical experience.", "example_zh": "\u35442 \u23416 \u29983 \u30003 \u35531 \u23559 \u22823 \u23416 \u20837 \u23416 \u26178 \u38291 \u25512 \u36978 \u19968 \u24180 \u65292 \u20197 \u20415 \u24037 \u20316 \u20006 \u29554 \u24471 \u23526 \u36368 \u32147 \u39511 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "defer a payment", "defer to authority", "defer admission", "defer a decision", "deferred gratification" ], "synonyms": ["postpone", "delay", "adjourn", "yield", "submit"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "postpone", "difference": "postpone strictly means to reschedule an event to a later calendar date; defer can mean delaying an action or, completely separately, yielding respectfully to someone else's superior judgment." \}, \{ "word": "yield", "difference": "yield means to give up or surrender under pressure or force, whereas defer to implies doing so out of voluntary respect." \} ], "antonyms": ["advance", "expedite", "resist", "defy"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "defer", "pos": "verb" \}, \{ "word": "deference", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "deferential", "pos": "adjective" \}, \{ "word": "deferment", "pos": "noun" \} ], "usage_note": "This word has two completely different meanings based on context: 1. To delay (takes an object: 'defer payment'). 2. To yield respectfully (takes 'to': 'defer to the doctor's advice').", "frequency_rank": 98, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "business", "academic"] \}, \{ "word": "deficient", "pos": "adjective", "phonetic": "/d\u618 \u712 f\u618 \u643 nt/", "definition": "Not having enough of a specified quality or ingredient; inadequate or incomplete.", "definition_zh": "\u32570 \u20047 \u30340 \u65307 \u26377 \u32570 \u38519 \u30340 \u65307 \u19981 \u22816 \u30340 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "de-", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "away, down", "meaning_zh": "\u21521 \u19979 \u12289 \u38626 \u38283 \u12289 \u19981 " \}, \{ "root": "facere / fici", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "to do, make", "meaning_zh": "\u20570 \u12289 \u35069 \u36896 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "DE\u65288 \u21521 \u19979 \u12289 \u19981 \u65289 + FACERE\u65288 \u20570 \u65289 \u8594  \u27794 \u26377 \u20570 \u21040 \u27161 \u28310 \u32218 \u20197 \u19978 \u30340  \u8594  \u32570 \u20047 \u19988 \u19981 \u21512 \u26684 \u30340 ", "story_en": "The concrete structure was severely DEFICIENT, because the builders did (fici) a bad job that fell DE- (down) below safety regulations.", "story_zh": "\u36889 \u20491 \u28151 \u20957 \u22303 \u32080 \u27083 \u26377 \u22196 \u37325 \u30340 \u12300 \u32570 \u38519 \u65288 DEFICIENT\u65289 \u12301 \u65292 \u22240 \u28858 \u24314 \u36896 \u32773 \u30340 \u24037 \u34269 \u20570 \u65288 FICI\u65289 \u24471 \u22826 \u24046 \u65292 \u38477 \u65288 DE\u65289 \u21040 \u20102 \u23433 \u20840 \u35215 \u31456 \u20197 \u19979 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55357 \u56521 ", "\u55356 \u57163 ", "\u55358 \u56817 "] \}, "example": "A diet that is deficient in essential vitamins can lead to severe health complications over time.", "example_zh": "\u38263 \u26399 \u32570 \u20047 \u24517 \u38656 \u32173 \u29983 \u32032 \u30340 \u39154 \u39135 \u21487 \u33021 \u26371 \u23566 \u33268 \u22196 \u37325 \u30340 \u20581 \u24247 \u20341 \u30332 \u30151 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "vitamin deficient", "mentally deficient", "deficient in resources", "severely deficient", "deficient performance" ], "synonyms": ["inadequate", "insufficient", "lacking", "flawed", "scanty"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "insufficient", "difference": "insufficient means simply not enough in numerical quantity (e.g., insufficient funds); deficient implies a structural flaw, weakness, or missing an essential quality/nutrient." \}, \{ "word": "scarce", "difference": "scarce means rare or hard to find in the market due to a general low supply." \} ], "antonyms": ["sufficient", "adequate", "abundant", "surplus"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "deficient", "pos": "adjective" \}, \{ "word": "deficiency", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "deficit", "pos": "noun" \} ], "usage_note": "Deficient is usually paired with the preposition 'in' (e.g., 'deficient in iron'). The noun form 'deficit' is heavily used in finance for budget shortages.", "frequency_rank": 99, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "science", "academic"] \}, \{ "word": "demonstrate", "pos": "verb", "phonetic": "/\u712 dem\u601 nstre\u618 t/", "definition": "To clearly show the existence or truth of something by giving proof or evidence; to give a practical exhibition and explanation of how something works.", "definition_zh": "\u35657 \u26126 \u65307 \u35542 \u35657 \u65307 \u31034 \u31684 \u65307 \u23637 \u31034 \u65307 \u36938 \u34892 \u31034 \u23041 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "de-", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "completely", "meaning_zh": "\u23436 \u20840 \u22320 \u12289 \u24505 \u24213 \u22320 " \}, \{ "root": "monstrare", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "to show, point out", "meaning_zh": "\u23637 \u31034 \u12289 \u25351 \u20986 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "DE\u65288 \u24505 \u24213 \u65289 + MONSTRARE\u65288 \u23637 \u31034 \u65289 \u8594  \u24505 \u24213 \u25351 \u20986 \u20358 \u32102 \u25152 \u26377 \u20154 \u30475  \u8594  \u35657 \u26126 \u25110 \u31034 \u31684 ", "story_en": "The chef will DEMONSTRATE the recipe, completely MONSTRARE (showing) how to slice the fish to the apprentice.", "story_zh": "\u20027 \u24282 \u23559 \u12300 \u31034 \u31684 \u65288 DEMONSTRATE\u65289 \u12301 \u36889 \u36947 \u33756 \u30340 \u20570 \u27861 \u65292 \u21521 \u23416 \u24466 \u24505 \u24213 \u23637 \u31034 \u65288 MONSTRARE\u65289 \u22914 \u20309 \u20999 \u39770 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55358 \u56785 \u8205 \u55356 \u57323 ", "\u55358 \u56810 ", "\u55357 \u56546 "] \}, "example": "The research results clearly demonstrate that the new teaching method significantly improves student engagement.", "example_zh": "\u30740 \u31350 \u32080 \u26524 \u28165 \u26970 \u22320 \u35657 \u26126 \u65292 \u26032 \u30340 \u25945 \u23416 \u26041 \u27861 \u39023 \u33879 \u25552 \u39640 \u20102 \u23416 \u29983 \u30340 \u21443 \u33287 \u24230 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "demonstrate competence", "clearly demonstrate", "demonstrate a product", "demonstrate against the war", "ability to demonstrate" ], "synonyms": ["prove", "showcase", "illustrate", "manifest", "protest"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "prove", "difference": "prove focuses on establishing logical or legal absolute truth; demonstrate emphasizes showing the process visually, practically, or step-by-step to make it clear." \}, \{ "word": "illustrate", "difference": "illustrate means to make something clear by using examples, charts, pictures, or stories." \} ], "antonyms": ["disprove", "conceal", "hide", "refute"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "demonstrate", "pos": "verb" \}, \{ "word": "demonstration", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "demonstrative", "pos": "adjective" \} ], "frequency_rank": 100, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "academic", "business"] \} ]}